WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.220
Let's kick things off with a quiz. Suppose I&nbsp;
take a normal distribution with this familiar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.220 --> 00:00:10.800
bell curve shape, and I have a random variable&nbsp;
X that's drawn from that distribution. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:15.780
what you're looking at right now are repeated&nbsp;
samples of that random variable and as a quick&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.780 --> 00:00:20.100
reminder, the way that you interpret this&nbsp;
curve, what the function X actually means&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:25.020
is that if you want the probability that your&nbsp;
sample falls within a given range of values,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.020 --> 00:00:29.580
say the probability that it ends up between&nbsp;
negative one and two, well, that would equal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.580 --> 00:00:34.800
the area under this curve in that range of&nbsp;
values. That's what the curve actually means.

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:40.200
I'll also pull up a second random variable also&nbsp;
following a normal distribution but may be this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:43.980
time a little more spread out, a slightly&nbsp;
bigger standard deviation and here's the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.980 --> 00:00:48.840
quiz for you - If you repeatedly sample both&nbsp;
of these variables and in each iteration you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.840 --> 00:00:53.520
add up the two results, well, then that&nbsp;
sum behaves like its own random variable.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:58.680
And the question is what distribution&nbsp;
describes that sum that you're looking at.

00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:03.180
You think about it for a little moment... Maybe&nbsp;
you have a guess. Maybe you think, "I don't know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.180 --> 00:01:08.700
it's another normal distribution or something with&nbsp;
a different shape." Needless to say, guessing is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:14.220
not enough. The real quiz is to be able to&nbsp;
explain why you get the answer that you do.

00:01:14.220 --> 00:01:18.840
In this case, if you have that deep to&nbsp;
your bones visceral level of understanding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.840 --> 00:01:23.400
for why the answer is what it is, you'll&nbsp;
be a long way towards understanding why&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:27.240
normal distributions serve the special&nbsp;
function that they do in probability.

00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:32.400
Zooming out though, this is actually meant to&nbsp;
be a much more general lesson about how you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:35.700
add two different random variables&nbsp;
regardless of their distribution,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.700 --> 00:01:38.400
not necessarily just the&nbsp;
normally distributed ones.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.060 --> 00:01:44.040
This amounts to a special operation that you&nbsp;
apply to the distributions underlying those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.040 --> 00:01:49.320
variables. The operation has a special name it's&nbsp;
called a convolution and the primary thing you and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:54.780
I will do today is motivate and build up two&nbsp;
distinct ways to visualize what a convolution&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.780 --> 00:01:59.640
looks like for continuous functions and then to&nbsp;
talk about these two different visualizations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.640 --> 00:02:04.740
can each be helpful in different ways with a&nbsp;
special focus on the central limit theorem.

00:02:04.740 --> 00:02:09.480
After we do the general lesson, I want to&nbsp;
return to the opening quiz and offer an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.480 --> 00:02:14.340
unusually satisfying way to answer it. As&nbsp;
a quick side note - regular viewers among&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.340 --> 00:02:18.240
you might know there's already a video&nbsp;
about convolutions on this channel. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:22.260
that one had a pretty different focus. We were&nbsp;
only doing the discrete case and I wanted to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.260 --> 00:02:26.220
show not just probability but the ways that&nbsp;
it comes up in a wide variety of contexts.

00:02:26.760 --> 00:02:30.360
I mean, a slightly awkward spot because it&nbsp;
doesn't really make sense for that to be a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.360 --> 00:02:35.520
prerequisite to this video but I think the best&nbsp;
way to warm up today is to cover essentially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.520 --> 00:02:39.660
one of the same examples used in that video.&nbsp;
So, if you are coming straight from that one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.660 --> 00:02:43.080
you can probably skip safely ahead.&nbsp;
Otherwise, let's dive right in.

00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:52.740
For this opening quiz question, each of the&nbsp;
random variables can take on a value in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.740 --> 00:02:57.900
continuous infinite range of values, all possible&nbsp;
real numbers. It'll be a lot easier if we warm up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.900 --> 00:03:02.760
in a setting that's more discreet and finite.&nbsp;
Like may be rolling a pair of weighted dice.

00:03:03.360 --> 00:03:07.260
Here, the animation you're looking&nbsp;
at is simulating two weighted dice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.260 --> 00:03:11.220
and you can probably tell what's going&nbsp;
on but just to spell it out explicitly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.220 --> 00:03:16.320
the blue die is following a distribution that&nbsp;
seems to be biased towards lower values. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.320 --> 00:03:21.360
red die has a distinct distribution and&nbsp;
then repeatedly sampling from each one and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:26.100
recording the sum of the two values at each&nbsp;
iteration. Repeating samples like this many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.100 --> 00:03:30.780
many different times can give you a heuristic&nbsp;
sense of the final distribution. But our real&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.780 --> 00:03:35.220
task today is to compute that distribution&nbsp;
precisely. What is the precise probability&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.220 --> 00:03:40.380
of rolling a two or a three or a four or&nbsp;
a five on and on for all possibilities.

00:03:40.380 --> 00:03:43.860
It's not too hard to question. And&nbsp;
actually encourage you to pause and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.860 --> 00:03:47.760
try working it out for yourself. The&nbsp;
main goal in this warm up section will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:52.620
be to walk through two distinct ways that you&nbsp;
could visualize the underlying computation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:59.040
For example, one way you could start to think&nbsp;
about it is that there are 36 distinct possible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.040 --> 00:04:03.480
outcomes and we could organize those&nbsp;
outcomes in a little six by six grid.

00:04:03.480 --> 00:04:09.360
Now, if I was to ask you what is the probability&nbsp;
of seeing any one of these specific outcomes? Say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.360 --> 00:04:14.400
the probability of seeing a blue four and&nbsp;
a red two, what would you say? We might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.400 --> 00:04:19.740
say it should be the probability of that blue&nbsp;
four multiplied by the probability of the red two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.740 --> 00:04:24.000
and that would be correct assuming that the&nbsp;
die rolls are independent from each other.

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:27.840
You might say that's kind of pedantic, of&nbsp;
course the die rolls should be independent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.840 --> 00:04:31.680
from each other. But it's a point worth&nbsp;
emphasizing because everything that we're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.680 --> 00:04:34.980
going to do from here moving forward,&nbsp;
from this simple example all the way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.980 --> 00:04:39.240
up to the central limit theorem assumes&nbsp;
that the random variables are independent.

00:04:39.240 --> 00:04:43.800
In the real world, you want to keep a sharp&nbsp;
eye out for if this assumption actually holds.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.340 --> 00:04:49.140
Now, what I'm going to do is take this grid of&nbsp;
all possible outcomes but start filling it in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.140 --> 00:04:53.400
with some numbers. Maybe we'll put the numbers&nbsp;
for all the probabilities of the blue die down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.400 --> 00:04:57.900
on the bottom, all the probabilities for the&nbsp;
red die over here on the left and then we will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.900 --> 00:05:03.000
fill in the grid where the probability for every&nbsp;
outcome inside the grid looks like some product&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.000 --> 00:05:07.080
between one number from the blue distribution&nbsp;
and one number from the red distribution.

00:05:07.080 --> 00:05:10.740
Another way to think about it is we're&nbsp;
basically constructing a multiplication&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.740 --> 00:05:15.360
table. To be a little more visual about all&nbsp;
of this, we could plot each one of these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.360 --> 00:05:20.760
probabilities as the height of a bar above the&nbsp;
square in this sort of three dimensional plot.

00:05:20.760 --> 00:05:24.420
In some sense, this three dimensional&nbsp;
plot carries all the data that we would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.420 --> 00:05:28.020
need to know about rolling a pair&nbsp;
of dice. And so, the question is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.020 --> 00:05:32.880
how do we extract the thing that we want to know,&nbsp;
the probabilities for various different sums?

00:05:34.320 --> 00:05:40.560
Well, if you highlight all of the outcomes with a&nbsp;
certain sum, say a sum of six, notice how all of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.560 --> 00:05:45.300
those end up on a certain diagonal. Same deal&nbsp;
if I highlight all the pairs where the sum is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.300 --> 00:05:50.760
seven. They sit along a different diagonal. So,&nbsp;
to compute the probability of each possible sum,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.760 --> 00:05:55.740
what you do is you add together all of the&nbsp;
entries that sit on one of these diagonals.

00:05:58.980 --> 00:06:04.140
Pulling up the 3D plot, we can better foreshadow&nbsp;
where we'll go with this later by saying that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.140 --> 00:06:09.120
distribution of possible sums looks like&nbsp;
combining all of the heights of this plot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.120 --> 00:06:14.460
along one of these diagonal slices. It's&nbsp;
as if we've taken this full distribution&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.460 --> 00:06:18.840
for all possible outcomes and we've kind of&nbsp;
collapsed it along one of the directions.

00:06:20.820 --> 00:06:24.900
And admittedly, I'm just having a bit of fun&nbsp;
with the animations at this point. It's not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.900 --> 00:06:27.840
like if you are working this out with&nbsp;
pencil and paper you would be drawing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.840 --> 00:06:31.560
some three dimensional plot. But it's fun&nbsp;
when you collapse it on this direction,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.560 --> 00:06:36.420
you actually do get the same distribution. Which&nbsp;
I knew you should but it's still fun to see.

00:06:36.420 --> 00:06:41.340
Also, even though all of this might just seem&nbsp;
a little bit playful or even unnecessarily&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.340 --> 00:06:46.500
complicated, I can promise you this intuition&nbsp;
about diagonal slices will come back to us later&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.500 --> 00:06:51.900
for a genuinely satisfying proof. But staying&nbsp;
focused on the simple dice case a little bit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.900 --> 00:06:56.460
longer, here's the second way that we could&nbsp;
think about it. Take that bottom distribution&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.460 --> 00:07:01.860
and flip it around horizontally so that the die&nbsp;
values increase as you go from right to left.

00:07:03.000 --> 00:07:08.220
Why do this you might ask? Well, notice now&nbsp;
which of the pairs of dice values line up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.220 --> 00:07:13.020
with each other. As it's positioned right&nbsp;
now, we have one and six, two and five,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.020 --> 00:07:18.600
three and four and so on. It is all of&nbsp;
the pairs of values that add up to seven.

00:07:18.600 --> 00:07:22.200
So if you want to think about the&nbsp;
probability of rolling a seven,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.200 --> 00:07:27.300
a way to hold that computation in your mind&nbsp;
is to take all of the pairs of probabilities&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.300 --> 00:07:32.580
that line up with each other, multiply together&nbsp;
those pairs and then add up all of the results.

00:07:33.300 --> 00:07:38.700
Some of you might like think of this as a kind of&nbsp;
dot product. But the operation as a whole is not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.700 --> 00:07:43.440
just one dot product but many. If we were to&nbsp;
slide that bottom distribution a little more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.440 --> 00:07:48.360
to the left - so, in this case it looks like&nbsp;
the die values which line up are one and four,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.360 --> 00:07:53.280
two and three, three and two, four and one - in&nbsp;
other words, all the ones that add up to a five,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.280 --> 00:07:58.560
well, now if we take the dot product, we&nbsp;
multiply the pairs of probabilities that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.560 --> 00:08:03.060
line up and add them together, that would give&nbsp;
us the total probability of rolling a five.

00:08:03.060 --> 00:08:08.520
In general, from this point of view, computing&nbsp;
the full distribution for the sum looks like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.520 --> 00:08:12.900
sliding that bottom distribution into various&nbsp;
different positions and computing this dot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.900 --> 00:08:19.860
product along the way. It is precisely the same&nbsp;
operation as the diagonal slices we were looking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.860 --> 00:08:24.300
at earlier. They're just two different ways&nbsp;
to visualise the same underlying operation.

00:08:27.120 --> 00:08:32.340
And however you choose to visualize&nbsp;
it, this operation that takes in two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.340 --> 00:08:37.140
different distributions and spits out a new&nbsp;
one describing the sum of the relevant random&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.140 --> 00:08:42.540
variables is called a convolution and we often&nbsp;
denote it with this asterisk. Really the way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.540 --> 00:08:45.960
you want to think about it, especially&nbsp;
as we set up for the continuous case is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.960 --> 00:08:49.800
to think of it as combining two different&nbsp;
functions and spitting out a new function.

00:08:50.640 --> 00:08:55.020
For example, in this case, maybe I give the&nbsp;
function for the first distribution the name&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.020 --> 00:09:00.900
P sub X. This would be a function that takes&nbsp;
in a possible value for the die, like a three&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.900 --> 00:09:07.740
and it spits out the corresponding probability.&nbsp;
Similarly, let's let P sub Y be the function for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.740 --> 00:09:13.620
our second distribution and P sub X plus Y be the&nbsp;
function describing the distribution for the sum.

00:09:14.280 --> 00:09:18.900
In the lingo, what you would say is that&nbsp;
P sub X plus Y is equal to a convolution&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.900 --> 00:09:24.300
between P sub X and P sub Y. And what&nbsp;
I want you to think about now is what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.300 --> 00:09:27.840
the formula for this operation should&nbsp;
look like. You've seen two different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.840 --> 00:09:31.200
ways to visualize it but how do we&nbsp;
actually write it down in symbols?

00:09:31.200 --> 00:09:35.460
To get your bearings, maybe it's helpful to&nbsp;
write down a specific example like the case&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.460 --> 00:09:40.140
of plugging in a four where you add up over all&nbsp;
the different pair wise products corresponding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.140 --> 00:09:44.880
to pairs of inputs that add up to a four&nbsp;
and more generally, here's how it might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.880 --> 00:09:49.860
look... This new function takes as an input&nbsp;
a possible sum for your random variables,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.860 --> 00:09:55.440
which I'll call "S" and what it outputs looks&nbsp;
like a sum over a bunch of pairs of values for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.440 --> 00:10:00.840
X and Y except the usual way it's written is&nbsp;
not to write with X and Y but instead we just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.840 --> 00:10:05.640
focus on one of those variables, in this case&nbsp;
X letting it range over all of its possible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.640 --> 00:10:10.500
values which here just means going from one all&nbsp;
the way up to six. And instead of writing "Y",&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.500 --> 00:10:16.200
you write S minus X. Essentially, whatever the&nbsp;
number has to be to make sure the sum is S.

00:10:17.640 --> 00:10:21.720
Now, the astute among you might notice&nbsp;
a slightly weird quirk with the formula&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.720 --> 00:10:26.700
as it's written. For example, if you plug&nbsp;
in a given value like s = 4 four and you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.700 --> 00:10:31.500
unpack the sum letting X range over all the&nbsp;
possible values going from one up to six,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.500 --> 00:10:36.180
then sometimes that corresponding&nbsp;
Y value drops below the domain of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.180 --> 00:10:41.760
what we've explicitly defined. For example, you&nbsp;
plug in zero and negative one and negative two.

00:10:41.760 --> 00:10:46.680
It's not actually that big a deal. Essentially you&nbsp;
would just say all of these values are zero. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.680 --> 00:10:50.700
all these later terms don't get counted.&nbsp;
And that should kind of make sense - what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.700 --> 00:10:54.660
is the probability that the red dyed&nbsp;
rolls to become a negative one? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.660 --> 00:10:56.940
it's zero, that is an impossible outcome.

00:11:01.200 --> 00:11:05.820
As a next step, let's turn our attention&nbsp;
towards continuous distributions where your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.820 --> 00:11:10.560
random variable can take on values anywhere&nbsp;
in an infinite continuum, like all possible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.560 --> 00:11:15.300
real numbers. Maybe you're doing weather modeling&nbsp;
and trying to predict the temperature tomorrow at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.300 --> 00:11:19.620
noon. Or you're doing some financial projections&nbsp;
or maybe you're modelling the typical wait times&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.620 --> 00:11:23.700
before a bus arrives. There are all sorts of&nbsp;
things where you need to handle continuity.

00:11:23.700 --> 00:11:27.960
In all the graphs that we draw, the&nbsp;
X value still represents a possible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.960 --> 00:11:32.220
number that the random variable can take&nbsp;
on. But the interpretation of the Y axis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.220 --> 00:11:35.880
is a little bit different because no&nbsp;
longer does this represent probability,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.880 --> 00:11:41.220
instead the thing that we're graphing is what's&nbsp;
called "Probability Density. This is something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.220 --> 00:11:46.140
we've talked about before so you know the deal.&nbsp;
Essentially, the probability that a sample of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.140 --> 00:11:51.420
your variable falls within a given range looks&nbsp;
like the area under the curve in that range.

00:11:51.420 --> 00:11:56.400
The function describing this curve is commonly&nbsp;
called a "Probability Density Function" - a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.400 --> 00:12:01.560
common enough phrase that it's frequently just&nbsp;
given the abbreviation PDF and so the proper way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.560 --> 00:12:05.460
to write all of this down would be to say that&nbsp;
the probability that your sample falls within&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:05.460 --> 00:12:12.180
a given range looks like the integral of your PDF&nbsp;
- the probability density function in that range.

00:12:12.840 --> 00:12:17.760
As a general rule of thumb, anytime that you&nbsp;
see a sum in the discrete case, you would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:17.760 --> 00:12:24.060
use an integral in the continuous case. So, let's&nbsp;
think about what that means for our main example.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:24.060 --> 00:12:28.560
Let's say we have two different random variables&nbsp;
but this time each one will follow a continuous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:28.560 --> 00:12:34.200
distribution and we want to understand their sum&nbsp;
and the new distribution that describes that sum.

00:12:35.400 --> 00:12:39.780
You can probably already guess what the&nbsp;
formula will be just by analogy. Remember,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:39.780 --> 00:12:45.960
in the formula that we just wrote down where P sub&nbsp;
X is the function for the first variable and P sub&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:45.960 --> 00:12:50.940
Y is the function for the second variable, the&nbsp;
convolution between them, the thing describing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:50.940 --> 00:12:56.100
a sum of those variables itself looks like a sum&nbsp;
where we combine a bunch of pair wise products.

00:12:56.100 --> 00:13:01.380
The expression in the continuous case really&nbsp;
does look 100% analogous. It's just that we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:01.380 --> 00:13:06.900
swap out that sum for an integral. Sometimes when&nbsp;
students see the definition of a convolution out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:06.900 --> 00:13:11.340
of context, it can seem a little intimidating.&nbsp;
Hopefully, the analogy is enough to make it clear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:11.340 --> 00:13:15.360
but the continuous nature really does give&nbsp;
it a different flavor and it's worth taking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:15.360 --> 00:13:20.340
a couple minutes to think through what it means&nbsp;
on its own terms and so, I put together a little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:20.340 --> 00:13:25.440
interactive demo that helps unpack each part&nbsp;
of the expression and what it's really saying.

00:13:25.440 --> 00:13:31.500
For example, the first term in this integral is&nbsp;
F of X, which represents the density function&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:31.500 --> 00:13:36.300
for the first of the two random variables and&nbsp;
in this case, I'm choosing this sort of wedge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:36.300 --> 00:13:40.440
shaped function for that distribution&nbsp;
but it could be anything. Similarly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:40.440 --> 00:13:45.360
G represents the density function for the second&nbsp;
random variable for which I'm choosing this sort&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:45.360 --> 00:13:50.340
of double lump shaped distribution and in the same&nbsp;
way that earlier we went over all possible pairs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:50.340 --> 00:13:56.280
of dice values with a given sum, the way you want&nbsp;
to think about this integral is that what it wants&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:56.280 --> 00:14:03.060
to do is iterate overall possible pairs of values&nbsp;
X and Y that are constrained to a given sum, S.

00:14:03.060 --> 00:14:07.440
We don't really have great notation for&nbsp;
doing that symmetrically, so instead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:07.440 --> 00:14:11.880
the way we commonly write it down gives this&nbsp;
artificial emphasis to one of the variables,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:11.880 --> 00:14:16.800
in this case X where we let that value&nbsp;
X range over all possible real numbers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:16.800 --> 00:14:23.280
negative infinity up to infinity, and the&nbsp;
thing we plug into the function G is S minus X,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:23.280 --> 00:14:28.080
essentially whatever it has to be to make&nbsp;
sure that the sum is constrained to be S.

00:14:29.580 --> 00:14:34.800
So, for the demo, instead of graphing G&nbsp;
directly, I want to graph G of S minus X&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:34.800 --> 00:14:39.780
and you might ask yourself "what is that&nbsp;
look like?" Well, if you plug in negative&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:39.780 --> 00:14:44.160
X as the input that has the effect of&nbsp;
flipping around the graph horizontally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:44.760 --> 00:14:49.440
and then if we throw in this parameter, S,&nbsp;
treated as some kind of constant, that has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:49.440 --> 00:14:54.420
the effect of shifting the graph either left or&nbsp;
right depending on if S is positive or negative.

00:14:54.420 --> 00:14:59.220
In the demo, S is a parameter that I'll just&nbsp;
grab and shift around a little bit. The real&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:59.220 --> 00:15:03.840
fun comes from graphing the entire contents&nbsp;
of the integral, the product between these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:03.840 --> 00:15:09.420
two graphs. This is analogous to the list of&nbsp;
pairwise products that we saw earlier but in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:09.420 --> 00:15:13.620
this case, instead of adding up all of those&nbsp;
pairwise products, we want to integrate them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:13.620 --> 00:15:17.820
together which you would interpret as&nbsp;
the area underneath this product graph.

00:15:17.820 --> 00:15:20.220
As I shift around this value of S,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:20.220 --> 00:15:24.360
the shape of that product graph changes&nbsp;
and so does the corresponding area.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:27.420 --> 00:15:32.760
Keep in mind, for all three graphs on the&nbsp;
left, the input is X and the number S is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:32.760 --> 00:15:38.160
just a parameter. But for the final graph on the&nbsp;
right, for the resulting convolution itself, this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:38.160 --> 00:15:44.580
number S is the input to that function and the&nbsp;
corresponding output is whatever the area of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:44.580 --> 00:15:49.980
lower left graph is. Whatever the integral between&nbsp;
this combination of F and G turns out to be.

00:15:53.640 --> 00:15:58.200
Here, it might be helpful if we do a simple&nbsp;
example. Say where each of our two random&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:58.200 --> 00:16:03.000
variables follows a uniform distribution&nbsp;
between the values negative one half and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:03.000 --> 00:16:08.160
positive one half. So, what that looks&nbsp;
like is that our density functions each&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:08.160 --> 00:16:12.540
have this kind of top hat shape where&nbsp;
the graph equals one for all inputs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:12.540 --> 00:16:16.680
between negative one half and positive one&nbsp;
half and it equals zero everywhere else.

00:16:17.220 --> 00:16:23.040
The question as always is what should the&nbsp;
distribution for the sum look like. Well, let me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:23.040 --> 00:16:28.560
show how it looks inside our demo. In this case,&nbsp;
the product between the two graphs has a really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:28.560 --> 00:16:34.200
easy interpretation. It is one wherever the graphs&nbsp;
overlap with each other but zero everywhere else.

00:16:34.800 --> 00:16:40.260
So, if I slide this parameter S far enough to the&nbsp;
left that our top graphs don't overlap at all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:40.260 --> 00:16:45.900
then the product graph is zero everywhere and&nbsp;
that's a way of saying this is an impossible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:45.900 --> 00:16:49.860
sum to achieve. That should make sense&nbsp;
- each of the two variables can only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:49.860 --> 00:16:54.540
get as low as negative one half. So, the&nbsp;
sum could never get below negative one.

00:16:55.800 --> 00:16:59.520
As I start to slide S to the right and&nbsp;
the graphs overlap with each other,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:59.520 --> 00:17:05.700
the area increases linearly until the graphs&nbsp;
overlap entirely and it reaches a maximum.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:06.240 --> 00:17:10.560
And then after that point, it starts&nbsp;
to decrease linearly again which means&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:10.560 --> 00:17:14.100
that the distribution for the sum&nbsp;
takes on this kind of wedge shape.

00:17:15.420 --> 00:17:19.680
And I imagine this actually feels somewhat&nbsp;
familiar for anyone who's thought about a pair of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:19.680 --> 00:17:25.920
dice. That is, unweighted dice. There, if you add&nbsp;
up two different uniformly distributed variables,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:25.920 --> 00:17:30.720
then the distribution for the sum has&nbsp;
a certain wedge shape. Probabilities&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:30.720 --> 00:17:34.500
increase until they max out at a seven&nbsp;
and then they decrease back down again.

00:17:36.480 --> 00:17:41.640
Where this gets a lot more fun is if instead of&nbsp;
asking for a sum of two uniformly distributed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:41.640 --> 00:17:46.560
variables, I ask you what it looks like if we&nbsp;
add up three different uniformly distributed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:46.560 --> 00:17:51.780
variables. At first you might say "I don't know,&nbsp;
we need some new way to visualize combining three&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:51.780 --> 00:17:55.800
things instead of two", but really what&nbsp;
you can do here is think about the sum&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:55.800 --> 00:18:00.660
of the first two as their own variable which&nbsp;
we just figured out follows this wedge shape&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:00.660 --> 00:18:04.860
distribution and then take a convolution&nbsp;
between that and the top hat function.

00:18:04.860 --> 00:18:08.520
Pulling up the demo, here's what&nbsp;
that would look like. Once again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:08.520 --> 00:18:13.200
what makes the top hat function really&nbsp;
nice is that multiplying by it sort&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:13.200 --> 00:18:16.920
of has the effect of filtering&nbsp;
out values from the top graph.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:16.920 --> 00:18:21.900
The product on the bottom looks just like a copy&nbsp;
of the top graph but limited to a certain window.

00:18:22.560 --> 00:18:26.580
Again, as I slide this around left and&nbsp;
right and the area gets bigger and smaller,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:26.580 --> 00:18:31.620
the result maxes out in the middle but tapers&nbsp;
out to either side. Except this time it does so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:31.620 --> 00:18:37.560
more smoothly. It's kind of like we're taking a&nbsp;
moving average of that top left graph. Actually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:37.560 --> 00:18:42.600
It's more than just kind of, this literally&nbsp;
is a moving average of the top left graph.

00:18:42.600 --> 00:18:47.100
One thing you might think to do is take this&nbsp;
even further. The way we started was combining&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:47.100 --> 00:18:51.900
two top hat functions and we got this wedge&nbsp;
then we replaced the first function with that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:51.900 --> 00:18:56.940
wedge and then when we took the convolution,&nbsp;
we got this smoother shape describing a sum&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:56.940 --> 00:19:02.340
of three distinct uniform variables but&nbsp;
we could just repeat. Swap that out for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:02.340 --> 00:19:07.500
the top function and then convolve that&nbsp;
with the flat rectangular function and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:07.500 --> 00:19:12.660
whatever result we see should describe a sum&nbsp;
of four uniformly distributed random variables.

00:19:13.680 --> 00:19:17.280
Any of you who watched the video about the&nbsp;
central limit theorem should know what to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:17.280 --> 00:19:22.800
expect. As we repeat this process over and over,&nbsp;
the shape looks more and more like a bell curve.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:22.800 --> 00:19:27.720
Or to be more precise, at each iteration,&nbsp;
we should rescale the X axis to make sure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:27.720 --> 00:19:32.640
that the standard deviation is one because the&nbsp;
dominant effect of this repeated convolution,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:32.640 --> 00:19:38.040
the kind of repeated moving average process&nbsp;
is to flatten out the function over time. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:38.040 --> 00:19:42.300
in the limit, it just flattens out towards&nbsp;
zero. But rescaling is a way of saying "yeah&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:42.300 --> 00:19:46.260
yeah yeah, I know that it gets flatter&nbsp;
but the actual shape underlying it all?"

00:19:48.180 --> 00:19:52.680
The statement of the central limit theorem,&nbsp;
one of the coolest facts from probability&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:52.680 --> 00:19:57.240
is that you could have started with&nbsp;
essentially any distribution and this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:57.240 --> 00:20:02.340
still would have been true. That as you take&nbsp;
repeated convolutions like this representing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:02.340 --> 00:20:07.260
bigger and bigger sums of a given random&nbsp;
variable, then the distribution describing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:07.260 --> 00:20:12.240
that sum which might start off looking very&nbsp;
different from a normal distribution over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:12.240 --> 00:20:17.580
time smooths out more and more until it gets&nbsp;
arbitrarily close to a normal distribution.

00:20:17.580 --> 00:20:22.920
It's as if a bell curve is in some loose&nbsp;
manner of speaking the smoothest possible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:22.920 --> 00:20:27.060
distribution and attractive fix point&nbsp;
in the space of all possible functions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:27.060 --> 00:20:30.960
as we apply this process of repeated&nbsp;
smoothing through the convolution.

00:20:35.640 --> 00:20:40.560
Naturally, you might wonder why normal&nbsp;
distributions? Why dysfunction and not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:40.560 --> 00:20:45.360
some other one? There's a very good answer and&nbsp;
I think the most fun way to show the answer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:45.360 --> 00:20:51.480
is in the light of the last visualization that&nbsp;
will show for convolutions. Remember how in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:51.480 --> 00:20:56.640
discreet case the first of our two visualizations&nbsp;
involved forming this kind of multiplication table&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:56.640 --> 00:21:01.680
showing the probabilities for all possible&nbsp;
outcomes and adding up along the diagonals.

00:21:03.000 --> 00:21:07.860
You've probably guessed it by now but our last&nbsp;
step is to generalize this to the continuous case.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:08.580 --> 00:21:11.100
And it is beautiful but you&nbsp;
have to be a little bit careful.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:12.000 --> 00:21:15.840
Pulling up the same two functions&nbsp;
we had before, F of X and G of Y,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:15.840 --> 00:21:21.720
what in this case would be analogous to the grid&nbsp;
of possible pairs that we were looking at earlier?

00:21:22.560 --> 00:21:26.940
Well in this case, each of the variables&nbsp;
can take on any real number. So we want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:26.940 --> 00:21:31.560
to think about all possible pairs of real&nbsp;
numbers and the XY plane comes to mind.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:32.700 --> 00:21:37.380
Every point corresponds to a possible outcome&nbsp;
when we sample from both distributions.

00:21:38.100 --> 00:21:42.780
Now, the probability of any one of these&nbsp;
outcomes, X Y or rather the probability&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:42.780 --> 00:21:49.140
density around that point will look like F of&nbsp;
X times G of Y. Again, assuming that the two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:49.140 --> 00:21:55.560
are independent. So a natural thing to do is to&nbsp;
graph this function, F of X times G of Y as a two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:55.560 --> 00:22:00.180
variable function. Which would give something&nbsp;
that looks like a surface above the X Y plane.

00:22:00.180 --> 00:22:05.820
Notice in this example how if we look at it from&nbsp;
one angle where we see the X values changing, it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:05.820 --> 00:22:10.620
has the shape of our first graph. But if we look&nbsp;
at it from another angle, emphasizing the change&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:10.620 --> 00:22:16.560
in the Y direction, it takes on the shape of our&nbsp;
second graph. This three dimensional graph encodes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:16.560 --> 00:22:21.360
all of the information we need. It shows all the&nbsp;
probability densities for every possible outcome.

00:22:21.900 --> 00:22:27.240
And if you want to limit your view just to&nbsp;
those outcomes where X plus Y is constrained&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:27.240 --> 00:22:31.500
to be a given sum, what that looks like&nbsp;
is limiting our view to a diagonal slice.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:31.500 --> 00:22:36.960
Specifically a slice over the line X plus&nbsp;
Y equals some constant. All of the possible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:36.960 --> 00:22:42.180
probability density for the outcome subject&nbsp;
to this constraint looks sort of like a slice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:42.180 --> 00:22:46.800
under this graph and as we change around&nbsp;
what specific sum we're constraining to,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:46.800 --> 00:22:50.700
it shifts around which specific&nbsp;
diagonal slice we're looking at.

00:22:54.420 --> 00:22:59.280
Now, what you might predict is that the way to&nbsp;
combine all of the probability densities along&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:59.280 --> 00:23:04.980
one of these slices, the way to integrate them&nbsp;
together can be interpreted as the area under&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:04.980 --> 00:23:11.160
this curve which is a slice of the surface. And&nbsp;
that is almost correct. There's a subtle detail&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:11.160 --> 00:23:16.020
regarding a factor of the square root of two that&nbsp;
we need to talk about but up to a constant factor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:16.020 --> 00:23:20.760
the areas of these slices give&nbsp;
us the values of the convolution.

00:23:20.760 --> 00:23:23.820
In fact, all of these slices that we're looking at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:23.820 --> 00:23:28.500
are precisely the same as the product&nbsp;
graph that we were looking at earlier.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:29.520 --> 00:23:35.100
Here, to emphasize this point, let me pull up&nbsp;
both visualizations side by side and I'm going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:35.100 --> 00:23:41.460
to slowly decrease the value of S from two down&nbsp;
to negative two, which on the left means we are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:41.460 --> 00:23:46.740
looking at different slices and on the right means&nbsp;
we're shifting around the modified graph of G.

00:23:47.340 --> 00:23:51.060
Notice how it all points the shape&nbsp;
of the graph on the bottom right,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:51.060 --> 00:23:56.820
the product between the functions, looks exactly&nbsp;
the same as the shape of the diagonal slice.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:58.920 --> 00:24:04.140
And this should make sense. They are two distinct&nbsp;
ways to visualise the same thing. It sounds like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:04.140 --> 00:24:09.000
a lot when we put it into words but what we're&nbsp;
looking at are all the possible products between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:09.000 --> 00:24:15.960
outputs of the functions corresponding to pairs of&nbsp;
inputs that have a given sum. Again, it's kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:15.960 --> 00:24:20.700
a mouthful but I think you see what I'm saying&nbsp;
and we now have two different ways to see it.

00:24:31.440 --> 00:24:35.220
The nice thing about the diagonal slice&nbsp;
visualization is that it makes it much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:35.220 --> 00:24:40.020
more clear that it's a symmetric operation.&nbsp;
It's much more obvious that F convolved with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:40.020 --> 00:24:47.400
G is the same thing as G convolved with F. Now,&nbsp;
technically, the diagonal slices are not exactly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:47.400 --> 00:24:51.480
the same shape. They've actually been stretched&nbsp;
out by a factor of the square root of two.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:52.200 --> 00:24:57.720
The basic reason is that if you imagine taking&nbsp;
some small step along one of these lines where X&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:57.720 --> 00:25:04.260
plus Y equals a constant, then the change in your&nbsp;
X value, that delta X here, is not the same thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:04.260 --> 00:25:09.420
as the length of that step. That step is actually&nbsp;
longer by a factor of the square root of two.

00:25:09.420 --> 00:25:14.220
I will leave a note up on the screen for the&nbsp;
calculus enthusiast among you who want to pause&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:14.220 --> 00:25:19.500
and ponder but the upshot is very simply that the&nbsp;
outputs of our convolution are technically not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:19.500 --> 00:25:24.600
quite the areas of these diagonal slices. You have&nbsp;
to divide those areas by a square root of two.

00:25:26.640 --> 00:25:28.440
Stepping back from all of this for a moment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:28.440 --> 00:25:33.660
I just think this is so beautiful. We started&nbsp;
with such a simple question or at least such&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:33.660 --> 00:25:38.400
a seemingly simple question - how do you add&nbsp;
up two random variables? And what we end up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:38.400 --> 00:25:42.360
with is this very intricate operation&nbsp;
for combining two different functions.

00:25:42.960 --> 00:25:46.800
We have at least two very pretty&nbsp;
ways to understand it. But still,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:46.800 --> 00:25:50.760
some of you might be raising your hands and&nbsp;
saying "Pretty pictures are all well and good,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:50.760 --> 00:25:55.140
but do they actually help you calculate&nbsp;
something?" For example, I still have not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:55.140 --> 00:25:59.940
answered the opening quiz question about adding&nbsp;
two normally distributed random variables.

00:26:00.480 --> 00:26:05.160
Well, the ordinary way that you would approach&nbsp;
this kind of question if it showed up on a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:05.160 --> 00:26:09.660
homework or something like that is that you would&nbsp;
plug in the formula for a normal distribution&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:09.660 --> 00:26:14.280
into the definition of a convolution - the&nbsp;
integral that we've been describing here.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:15.420 --> 00:26:19.260
And in that case, the visualizations&nbsp;
would really just be there to clarify&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:19.260 --> 00:26:22.020
what the expression is saying&nbsp;
but they sit in the back seat.

00:26:22.020 --> 00:26:26.880
In this case, the integral is not prohibitively&nbsp;
difficult - there are analytical methods,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:26.880 --> 00:26:31.920
but for this example, I want to show you a&nbsp;
more fun method where the visualizations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:31.920 --> 00:26:37.500
specifically the diagonal slices, will play a much&nbsp;
more front and center role in the proof itself.

00:26:38.220 --> 00:26:42.240
I think many of you may actually enjoy taking&nbsp;
a moment to predict how this will look for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:42.240 --> 00:26:47.040
yourself. Think about what this 3D graph&nbsp;
would look like in the case of two normal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:47.040 --> 00:26:51.840
distributions and what properties that it has&nbsp;
that you might be able to take advantage of.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:52.980 --> 00:26:57.420
And it is for sure easiest if you start with&nbsp;
the case where both distributions have the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:57.420 --> 00:27:02.820
same standard deviation. Whenever you want&nbsp;
the details and to see how the answer fits&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:02.820 --> 00:27:05.400
into the central limit theorem,&nbsp;
come join me in the next video.

